@prefix ldh:    <https://w3id.org/atomgraph/linkeddatahub#> .
@prefix rdf:    <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#> .
@prefix dh:     <https://w3id.org/atomgraph/linkeddatahub/document-hierarchy#> .
@prefix dct:    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/> .

<> a dh:Item ;
    dct:title "Connect an agent" ;
    dct:description "Give an AI agent such as Claude Code or Cursor access to a LinkedDataHub instance, with the ldh Claude Code plugin or over MCP with Web-Algebra" ;
    rdf:_1 <#content> .

<#content> a ldh:XHTML ;
    rdf:value """<div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
    <p class="ldh-body-lg">Give an AI agent such as Claude Code or Cursor access to a LinkedDataHub instance, with the ldh Claude Code plugin or over MCP with Web-Algebra</p>
    <p>Agents use the same HTTP API as the browser and the <a href="../../reference/command-line-interface/">CLI</a>, and act as a WebID: an agent can read and
        write only what its WebID is authorized to. There are two ways to connect one. The <a href="#claude-code-plugin">Claude Code plugin</a> gives Claude Code
        skills for building on LinkedDataHub with the <samp>ldh</samp> CLI. <a href="#mcp">Web-Algebra</a> exposes LinkedDataHub operations to any
        <a href="https://modelcontextprotocol.io/" target="_blank">Model Context Protocol</a> (MCP) client as tools: creating containers and items, adding views,
        queries and charts, reading and writing RDF, and running SPARQL. Either way, an agent can build a working application in a dataspace:
        its classes, containers, views and charts are documents like any other.</p>
    <p>The conventions agents should follow when writing to LinkedDataHub are in
        <a href="https://github.com/AtomGraph/LinkedDataHub/blob/master/AGENTS.md" target="_blank">AGENTS.md</a>. To ask questions about the data in a document
        from the browser instead, use the <a href="../../user-guide/use-the-assistant/">assistant</a>.</p>
    <div>
        <h2 id="claude-code-plugin">Claude Code plugin</h2>
        <p>The <a href="https://github.com/AtomGraph/LinkedDataHub/tree/master/plugins/ldh" target="_blank"><samp>ldh</samp> plugin</a> ships with LinkedDataHub.
            Its marketplace is the LinkedDataHub repository, so Claude Code is told about it once and then installs the plugin from it. In Claude Code:</p>
        <pre>/plugin marketplace add AtomGraph/LinkedDataHub
/plugin install ldh@linkeddatahub</pre>
        <p>The skills drive the <samp>ldh</samp> CLI, so they need a LinkedDataHub instance, the owner's WebID certificate and the CLI on the <samp>PATH</samp>
            (see <a href="../../reference/command-line-interface/#install">installing the CLI</a>). Claude loads a skill by itself when a request matches what it
            covers. To call one explicitly, type <samp>/ldh:</samp> followed by its name.</p>
        <table>
            <caption class="ac-vh">Skills of the ldh plugin</caption>
            <thead>
                <tr>
                    <th scope="col">Skill</th>
                    <th scope="col">Covers</th>
                </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr>
                    <th scope="row"><samp>ldh:dataspace</samp></th>
                    <td>Provisioning a new dataspace on a self-hosted instance: the configuration files, the restart, and checking that it is up</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <th scope="row"><samp>ldh:dataset</samp></th>
                    <td>The namespace ontology and its install, CSV and RDF imports, federation with remote SPARQL endpoints, and building a large dataset standalone and bulk-loading it</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <th scope="row"><samp>ldh:app</samp></th>
                    <td>Documents, blocks, views and charts, and an app kept as a directory and installed with <samp>ldh push</samp></td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <th scope="row"><samp>ldh:package</samp></th>
                    <td>A reusable <a href="../../reference/administration/packages/">package</a>: an ontology and an XSLT stylesheet, published to a registry and imported with <samp>ldh packages add</samp></td>
                </tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
        <p>A request can span several skills, and Claude uses them in turn:</p>
        <blockquote><p>Here are products.csv and categories.csv. Model them with schema.org, import them into the inventory dataspace, and make each category's page list its products.</p></blockquote>
    </div>
    <div>
        <h2 id="mcp">Web-Algebra over MCP</h2>
        <p><a href="https://github.com/AtomGraph/Web-Algebra" target="_blank">Web-Algebra</a> expresses LinkedDataHub operations, SPARQL queries and Linked Data
            reads as plans that run as one unit, and its MCP server offers them as tools. Any MCP client can use it.</p>
    <div>
        <h3 id="prerequisites">Prerequisites</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>A running LinkedDataHub instance. The examples below assume the default <a href="../setup/">local setup</a> at <samp>https://localhost:4443/</samp>.</li>
            <li>The WebID certificate the agent will act as. For your own instance that is the owner certificate, <samp>ssl/owner/cert.pem</samp>, with the password from
                <samp>secrets/owner_cert_password.txt</samp>. The agent can do whatever that WebID is authorized to do.</li>
            <li><a href="https://docs.astral.sh/uv/getting-started/installation/" target="_blank">uv</a>, which runs Web-Algebra without a separate install step.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
    <div>
        <h3 id="claude-code">Claude Code</h3>
        <p>Register Web-Algebra as an MCP server, run from the LinkedDataHub directory:</p>
        <pre>claude mcp add web-algebra \\
  --env CERT_PEM_PATH="$PWD/ssl/owner/cert.pem" \\
  --env CERT_PASSWORD="$(cat secrets/owner_cert_password.txt)" \\
  -- uv run --with web-algebra python -m web_algebra</pre>
        <p>Start <samp>claude</samp> and check that <samp>/mcp</samp> lists <samp>web-algebra</samp> as connected.</p>
    </div>
    <div>
        <h3 id="cursor-and-claude-desktop">Cursor and Claude Desktop</h3>
        <p>Both read an <samp>mcpServers</samp> entry: <samp>~/.cursor/mcp.json</samp> for Cursor, <samp>claude_desktop_config.json</samp> for Claude Desktop.
            Use absolute paths and the literal password, since these files are not run by a shell.</p>
        <pre>{
  "mcpServers": {
    "web-algebra": {
      "command": "uv",
      "args": ["run", "--with", "web-algebra", "python", "-m", "web_algebra"],
      "env": {
        "CERT_PEM_PATH": "/path/to/LinkedDataHub/ssl/owner/cert.pem",
        "CERT_PASSWORD": "your-owner-cert-password"
      }
    }
  }
}</pre>
        <p>If the client cannot find <samp>uv</samp>, replace <samp>"uv"</samp> with its absolute path, as printed by <samp>which uv</samp>.</p>
    </div>
    <div>
        <h3 id="http-transport">Streamable HTTP transport</h3>
        <p>Clients that connect to an MCP URL instead of launching a process can use Web-Algebra's HTTP server:</p>
        <pre>CERT_PEM_PATH="$PWD/ssl/owner/cert.pem" \\
CERT_PASSWORD="$(cat secrets/owner_cert_password.txt)" \\
uv run --with web-algebra uvicorn web_algebra.server:app</pre>
        <p>The MCP URL is <samp>http://127.0.0.1:8000/mcp</samp>. For Claude Code:
            <code>claude mcp add --transport http web-algebra http://127.0.0.1:8000/mcp</code>.</p>
        <div class="ac-alert va-warning" role="status">
            <span class="ac-alert-ic"><span aria-hidden="true" class="msi outline">warning</span></span>
            <div class="ac-alert-body">
                <p class="ac-alert-text">The HTTP server does not authenticate its callers, and it holds your WebID certificate. Anyone who can reach the port acts as you.
                    Keep it bound to <samp>127.0.0.1</samp>, which is the default, and do not expose it on a network.</p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
    </div>
    <div>
        <h2 id="try-it">Try it</h2>
        <p>Ask the agent for something small that it has to declare rather than code. With the <a href="https://github.com/AtomGraph/LinkedDataHub-Apps/tree/master/demo/northwind-traders" target="_blank">Northwind Traders</a> demo installed, for example:</p>
        <blockquote><p>Create a container for new offers in my dataspace, add a 20% discount offer for three Beverages products, and add a chart of the offer prices.</p></blockquote>
        <p>Then open <samp>https://localhost:4443/</samp> in the browser. The container, the items and the chart are there, and you can keep editing them by hand —
            the agent and you work on the same documents.</p>
    </div>
</div>"""^^rdf:XMLLiteral .
